#
# CCIC devices
#

comment "CCIC configs"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# USE_CCIC: the S2MU106 USB-PD *framework* switch.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4.4 gates usbpd.o, usbpd_cc.o, usbpd_policy.o and usbpd_manager.o on
# USE_CCIC, and gates each chip on "depends on USE_CCIC". This tree had no
# USE_CCIC at all, which is why the framework had no home. The four framework
# .c files are now in the tree, so the symbol is added and the chip symbols are
# wired to it the way 4.4 wires them.
config USE_CCIC
	bool "Using USBPD device driver"
	depends on I2C
	default n
	help
	  If you say yes here you will get support for
	  the USB PD(Power Delivery) device driver.

	  This builds the chip-independent half of Samsung's Type-C PD stack:
	  usbpd.c (protocol layer), usbpd_cc.c (CC/PD glue and the mainline
	  Type-C adapter), usbpd_manager.c (PDO and capability management) and
	  usbpd_policy.c (the policy state machine). It is meaningless on its
	  own; a chip symbol such as CCIC_S2MU106 must also be selected.

config CCIC_S2MU106
	bool "Using S2MU106 USB PD"
	depends on USE_CCIC
	depends on I2C
	depends on !CCIC_S2MM005
	default n
	help
	  If you say yes here you will get support for
	  for the S2MU106 USBPD chip.

	  The !CCIC_S2MM005 line is mutual exclusion, not decoration.
	  drivers/ccic/s2mm005_pd.c:80 defines
	  vbus_turn_on_ctrl(struct s2mm005_data *, bool) -- actually
	  vbus_turn_on_ctrl(bool) -- while
	  drivers/ccic/s2mu106-usbpd.c:200 defines
	  vbus_turn_on_ctrl(struct s2mu106_usbpd_data *, bool). C has no
	  function overloading, so linking both objects is a duplicate-symbol
	  error at best and, in a translation unit that saw both declarations,
	  a conflicting-types error at compile time. Measured: no TU in this
	  tree sees both declarations, because
	  include/linux/ccic/s2mm005_ext.h (the 1-arg form) is included only by
	  s2mm005*.c, ccic_alternate.c and
	  drivers/usb/manager/usb_typec_manager_notifier.c, none of which is in
	  the S2MU106 include closure. So the collision is a LINK-time one and
	  excluding the combination here is sufficient.

	  This is also the correct configuration on its own merits: the real
	  A307FN device tree has no s2mm005 node anywhere, and the stock
	  4.4.177 config selects CCIC_S2MU106 and not CCIC_S2MM005.

# 4.4 defines these three in drivers/battery_v2/Kconfig. They are relocated
# here because their only consumer in the whole tree is
# drivers/ccic/usbpd_policy.c, and because putting them here lets the
# dependency on USE_CCIC be expressed. PDIC_PD30 in particular is
# load-bearing: 36 #if defined(CONFIG_PDIC_PD30) sites in usbpd_policy.c
# select between the PD 2.0 and PD 3.0 message sets, the RP180/RP330 current
# advertisement, and whether spec_revision is re-read from the wire. 4.4
# gives it "default y"; that default is reproduced, not weakened, and the
# stock device is PD 2.0 so the #else arms (USBPD_PD2_0, RP180) are what
# actually run in practice.
config PDIC_PD30
	bool "support for PDIC_30"
	depends on USE_CCIC
	default y
	help
	  Say Y to include support for PDIC 30. Relocated from 4.4's
	  drivers/battery_v2/Kconfig; only drivers/ccic/usbpd_policy.c uses it.

config CCIC_MANUAL_QBAT
	bool "Using S2MU106 USB PD QBAT Manual Control"
	depends on USE_CCIC
	default n
	help
	  If you say yes here you will get support for
	  for the S2MU106 USBPD chip.
	  Read by drivers/ccic/s2mu106-usbpd.c at lines 1814 and 1855, each
	  under "&& !defined(CONFIG_SEC_FACTORY)". The stock config sets
	  CONFIG_CCIC_MANUAL_QBAT=y.

config CCIC_TYPEC_PRSWAP
	bool "support Power Role Swap"
	depends on USE_CCIC
	depends on CCIC_S2MU106
	default y
	help
	  If you say yes here you will get support for
	  for the Power Role Swap.

	  4.4 adds two depends-on lines that are deliberately NOT reproduced:
	  "depends on ANDROID_MAJOR_VERSION != n/o/p" and
	  "depends on CCIC_S2MU004 || CCIC_S2MU106 || CCIC_S2MU205".

	  The chip list reduces to CCIC_S2MU106 because the other two chip
	  symbols are left undefined on purpose (see below).

	  The ANDROID_MAJOR_VERSION guard is a landmine. That symbol is an
	  env-provided string (Kconfig:15, "option env=") and the 4.9 port is
	  built standalone with plain make rather than through a ROM's soong
	  build, so it is normally empty -- and an empty string satisfies
	  != "n", != "o" and != "p", which is why the guard looks harmless
	  today. But a 4.9 kernel is an Android 9/10-era tree, so "p" is its
	  natural value; anyone building this with ANDROID_MAJOR_VERSION=p set
	  would silently lose Power Role Swap, which the stock device needs
	  because the port is a dual-role DRP. The tree's own
	  drivers/usb/typec/Kconfig already uses a single "!= o" guard for the
	  same reason. The stock config sets CONFIG_CCIC_TYPEC_PRSWAP=y.

# NOT added, on purpose. Leaving these four symbols undefined is what makes
# the correct thing happen if a defconfig names them:
#   CCIC_S2MU004  30 #if sites, and the only reason usbpd_cc.c would need
#                 include/linux/ccic/usbpd-s2mu004.h, which does not exist here
#   CCIC_S2MU205  26 #if sites, same, via usbpd-s2mu205.h
#   CCIC_VDM      4.4 says it is the S2MU004 VDM feature. Its only consumers
#                 tree-wide are inside drivers/ccic/s2mu004-usbpd.c, which is
#                 not part of this port. It is a no-op on S2MU106.
#   CCIC_MODE_BY_MUIC, CCIC_MODE_WITHOUT_MUIC, CCIC_EXTERNAL_CAPACITOR
#                 the stock config records CCIC_MODE_BY_MUIC as not set
# If any of them were defined, Kconfig would drop the "=y" from a defconfig
# with a warning rather than compiling an include that is not there.

config CCIC_PDIC_NOTIFIER
	bool "S2MU106 CCIC attach/detach notifier"
	depends on USE_MUIC
	default n
	help
	  The s2m_pdic_notifier_* attach/detach broadcast chain. This is the
	  notifier the S2MU106 MUIC driver publishes cable attach and detach
	  on, and it is what drivers/muic/s2mu106-muic.c and muic_manager.c
	  reach through MUIC_SEND_NOTI_TO_CCIC_ATTACH / _DETACH. It is not
	  part of USB-PD and is needed whether or not CONFIG_CCIC_S2MU106 is
	  set.
	  This is the s2m_ family and is distinct from the CCIC_NOTIFIER
	  chain, which serves the max77705 / s2mm005 CCIC parts.

# CONFIG_CCIC_S2MU106 is NOT wired to the USB-PD framework on purpose.
#
# The 4.4 tree gates the whole PD stack on a symbol called USE_CCIC, which
# builds usbpd.o, usbpd_cc.o, usbpd_policy.o, usbpd_manager.o, pdic_notifier.o
# and ccic_misc.o. No USE_CCIC symbol exists in this 4.9 tree, so setting
# CONFIG_CCIC_S2MU106 here would build s2mu106-usbpd.o with 12 unresolved
# usbpd_* entry points and fail at link.
#
# The measurement behind that decision, and the 4.14 evidence that Samsung
# deleted this stack, are in the port report.
#
# CORRECTION 2026-09-27 (CCIC/MUIC audit). An earlier revision of this comment
# justified leaving CCIC_S2MU106 off by claiming "the A30s has a micro-USB
# port ... USB-PD is a Type-C protocol". That reasoning was wrong, and the
# conclusion has to be re-derived from the link error alone, because the next
# wave will otherwise skip this subsystem as inapplicable hardware.
#
# The A30s is a USB Type-C phone and the PD stack is real. Three independent
# confirmations:
#   1. the live device has /sys/class/typec/port0, usb_typec_revision=1.2,
#      DEVTYPE=typec_port, dual source/sink;
#   2. the stock 4.4.177 A307FN config has CCIC_S2MU106=y and MUIC_S2MU106=y;
#   3. the stock bootloader passes ccic_info=0x1 in chosen/bootargs.
# The board device tree also carries usbpd_irq (gpa2-3), samsung,usb-notifier,
# samsung,sec_detect_conn and pdic,usb_com_capable = <0x1>.
#
# What is genuinely absent is a *mainline* Type-C connector node, because
# Samsung's own CCIC driver owns the port rather than drivers/usb/typec. That
# is why the Type-C hardware is invisible to a mainline reading of the device
# tree; it is NOT evidence that the port is not Type-C. Full USB-PD is
# therefore mandatory for this board, not optional.
#
# So CCIC_S2MU106 must stay n only until the USB-PD framework is ported: the
# framework contributes 6,212 + 1,659 + 757 + 651 = 9,279 lines across
# usbpd_policy.c, usbpd_manager.c, usbpd_cc.c and usbpd.c, plus 4.4's 412-line
# drivers/muic/muic_notifier.c for muic_ccic_notifier_register. On its own
# s2mu106-usbpd.o leaves 15 symbols unresolved. Manifest in the audit report.
config CCIC_NOTIFIER
	bool "CCIC notifier support"
	depends on I2C
	default n
	help
	  If you say yes here you will get support for
	  the CCIC attached device status change notification.

